{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "HDFC Bank vs PSU Bank: Residual Income & P/B Valuation",
  "scenario": "You're a Mumbai-desk analyst comparing HDFC Bank and Indian Bank (a PSU lender). Both are profitable, but HDFC trades at 3× book while Indian Bank trades at 0.5× book. Your task: compute residual income, ROE-Ke spreads, and justified P/B ratios to understand why the market values them so differently — and prove that value creation hinges entirely on returns above the cost of capital.",
  "instructions": "Complete the Answers sheet by computing residual income for each bank-year, the ROE-Ke spread, justified P/B multiples, and EVA. Use the DDM Gordon Growth formula to value Coal India's dividend stream. Trace every formula against the Data sheet.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute residual income, justified price-to-book ratios, and EVA to explain why banks trading above book create spreads above their cost of capital, and value Coal India using the dividend discount model.",
  "hints": [
    "Residual Income = Net Income − (Cost of Equity × Book Equity). Start with the simple subtraction in row 2.",
    "Justified P/B = (ROE − growth rate) / (Cost of Equity − growth rate). The numerator and denominator both involve the spread; ROE 17%, Ke 13%, g 10% means (0.17−0.10)/(0.13−0.10).",
    "For DDM: Value = Next Dividend / (Cost of Equity − growth). Coal India has ₹25 next dividend, Ke 12%, growth 5%; solve for share value. For EVA: NOPAT − (WACC × Invested Capital)."
  ],
